window.docContents[186].resolve({"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___11___-Tensor-products/Pure-tensors-and-tensor-products-of-multiple-spaces/#Chapter2___Discussion_pure_tensors___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.11. Tensor products\u0009Pure tensors and tensor products of multiple spaces\u0009Pure tensors and tensor products of multiple spaces","header":"Formalization","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___11___-Tensor-products/Pure-tensors-and-tensor-products-of-multiple-spaces/#Chapter2___Discussion_pure_tensors___formalization"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___16___-Problems-on-Lie-algebras/Lie___s-theorem___-irreducible-representations-of-solvable-Lie-algebras/#Chapter2___Problem2___16___1":{"contents":"Problem 2.16.1 (Lie's theorem). The commutant K(\\mathfrak{g}) of a Lie algebra \\mathfrak{g} is the linear span of elements [x, y], x, y \\in \\mathfrak{g}. This is an ideal in \\mathfrak{g} (i.e., it is a subrepresentation of the adjoint representation). A\nfinite dimensional Lie algebra \\mathfrak{g} over a field k is said to be solvable if there exists n such that K^n(\\mathfrak{g}) = 0. Prove the Lie theorem: if k = \\mathbb{C} and V is a finite dimensional irreducible representation of a solvable Lie algebra \\mathfrak{g}, then V is 1-dimensional.\n\nHint: Prove the result by induction in dimension. By the induction assumption, K(\\mathfrak{g}) has a common eigenvector v in V; that is, there is a linear function \\chi : K(\\mathfrak{g}) \\to \\mathbb{C} such that av = \\chi(a)v for any a \\in K(\\mathfrak{g}). Show that \\mathfrak{g} preserves common eigenspaces of K(\\mathfrak{g}). (For this you will need to show that \\chi([x, a]) = 0 for x \\in \\mathfrak{g} and a \\in K(\\mathfrak{g}). To prove this, consider the smallest subspace U containing v and invariant under x. This subspace is invariant under K(\\mathfrak{g}) and any a \\in K(\\mathfrak{g}) acts with trace \\dim(U)\\chi(a) in this subspace. In particular 0 = \\operatorname{Tr}([x, a]) = \\dim(U)\\chi([x, a]).)\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.16. Problems on Lie algebras\u0009Lie's theorem: irreducible representations of solvable Lie algebras","header":"Lie's theorem: irreducible representations of solvable Lie algebras","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___16___-Problems-on-Lie-algebras/Lie___s-theorem___-irreducible-representations-of-solvable-Lie-algebras/#Chapter2___Problem2___16___1"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___8___-The-Krull-Schmidt-theorem/The-Krull-Schmidt-theorem/#Chapter3___Theorem3___8___1":{"contents":"Theorem 3.8.1 (Krull-Schmidt theorem). Any finite dimensional representation of A can be uniquely (up to an isomorphism and the order of summands) decomposed into a direct sum of indecomposable representations.\n\nProof. It is clear that a decomposition of V into a direct sum of indecomposable representations exists, so we just need to prove uniqueness. We will prove it by induction on \\dim V. Let V = V_1 \\oplus \\cdots \\oplus V_m = V'_1 \\oplus \\cdots \\oplus V'_n. Let i_s : V_s \\to V, i'_s : V'_s \\to V, p_s : V \\to V_s, p'_s : V \\to V'_s be the natural maps associated with these decompositions. Let \\theta_s = p_1 i'_s p'_s i_1 : V_1 \\to V_1. We have \\sum_{s=1}^n \\theta_s = 1. Now we need the following lemma.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00093. General results of representation theory\u00093.8. The Krull-Schmidt theorem\u0009The Krull-Schmidt theorem","header":"The Krull-Schmidt theorem","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___8___-The-Krull-Schmidt-theorem/The-Krull-Schmidt-theorem/#Chapter3___Theorem3___8___1"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___9___-Problems/Irreducible-representations-and-Ext___1-for-path-algebras/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--3___-General-results-of-representation-theory--3___9___-Problems--Irreducible-representations-and-Ext___1-for-path-algebras--Irreducible-representations-and-Ext___1-for-path-algebras--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"Characterizes the displayed auxiliary relation on vertices by emptiness of the quiver hom type.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.9.3; role=supporting]\n\nUnder the displayed finiteness and auxiliary predicates, some vertex has a nonempty associated auxiliary object.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.9.3; role=supporting]\n\nThe dimension of the auxiliary type equals the cardinality of the quiver hom type.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.9.3; role=supporting]\n\nUnder the displayed quiver and dimension hypotheses, either the auxiliary predicate fails or a displayed map is bijective.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.9.3; role=supporting]\n\nAn auxiliary definition whose formal type is unavailable.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.9.3; role=supporting]\n\nAn auxiliary definition whose formal type is unavailable.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.9.3; role=supporting]\n\nAn auxiliary proposition whose formal type is unavailable.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.9.3; role=supporting]\n\nAn auxiliary proposition whose formal type is unavailable.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.9.3; role=supporting]\n\nAn auxiliary proposition whose formal type is unavailable.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.9.3; role=supporting]\n\nAn auxiliary proposition whose formal type is unavailable.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.9.3; role=supporting]\n\nThe fifth auxiliary definition at zero does not satisfy the displayed predicate.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.9.3; role=supporting]\n\nUnder the displayed quiver hypothesis, a representation of total dimension two either fails the displayed predicate or has a bijective arrow map between distinct vertices.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.9.3; role=supporting]\n\nDisplayed equivalence data between the fifth auxiliary definition at zero and the displayed\nconstruction on its two vertices.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Problem3.9.3; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00093. General results of representation theory\u00093.9. Problems\u0009Irreducible representations and Ext^1 for path algebras\u0009Irreducible representations and Ext^1 for path algebras\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___9___-Problems/Irreducible-representations-and-Ext___1-for-path-algebras/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--3___-General-results-of-representation-theory--3___9___-Problems--Irreducible-representations-and-Ext___1-for-path-algebras--Irreducible-representations-and-Ext___1-for-path-algebras--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___3___-Examples/Representations-of-finite-abelian-groups-and-dual-groups/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results--4___3___-Examples--Representations-of-finite-abelian-groups-and-dual-groups--Representations-of-finite-abelian-groups-and-dual-groups--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"The character group of a finite product is multiplicatively equivalent to the displayed product of character groups.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter4/Example4.3_FiniteAbelianGroups; role=primary]\n\nThe character group of a product is multiplicatively equivalent to the product of character groups.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter4/Example4.3_FiniteAbelianGroups; role=primary]\n\nA finite simple representation of a finite commutative group over an algebraically closed field has dimension one.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter4/Example4.3_FiniteAbelianGroups; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00094. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\u00094.3. Examples\u0009Representations of finite abelian groups and dual groups\u0009Representations of finite abelian groups and dual groups\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___3___-Examples/Representations-of-finite-abelian-groups-and-dual-groups/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results--4___3___-Examples--Representations-of-finite-abelian-groups-and-dual-groups--Representations-of-finite-abelian-groups-and-dual-groups--Formalization--Primary-declarations"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___12___-Representations-of-___S_n___/Specht-modules-for-partitions-_LPAR_n_RPAR____-_LPAR_1___n_RPAR____-and-small-n/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___12___-Representations-of-___S_n___--Specht-modules-for-partitions-_LPAR_n_RPAR____-_LPAR_1___n_RPAR____-and-small-n--Specht-modules-for-partitions-_LPAR_n_RPAR____-_LPAR_1___n_RPAR____-and-small-n--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"Every permutation fixes each vector in the subspace associated with the alternate auxiliary partition.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Example5.12.3; role=primary]\n\nA permutation acts on the auxiliary subtype by multiplication by its sign.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Example5.12.3; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.12. Representations of $S_n$\u0009Specht modules for partitions (n), (1^n), and small n\u0009Specht modules for partitions (n), (1^n), and small n\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___12___-Representations-of-___S_n___/Specht-modules-for-partitions-_LPAR_n_RPAR____-_LPAR_1___n_RPAR____-and-small-n/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___12___-Representations-of-___S_n___--Specht-modules-for-partitions-_LPAR_n_RPAR____-_LPAR_1___n_RPAR____-and-small-n--Specht-modules-for-partitions-_LPAR_n_RPAR____-_LPAR_1___n_RPAR____-and-small-n--Formalization--Primary-declarations"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___15___-The-Frobenius-character-formula/Partial-order-on-partitions-and-vanishing-of-Kostka-numbers/#Chapter5___Remark5___15___5":{"contents":"Remark 5.15.5. For partitions \\lambda and \\mu of n, let us say that \\lambda \\preceq \\mu or \\mu \\succeq \\lambda if \\mu - \\lambda is a sum of vectors of the form e_i - e_j, i < j (called positive roots). This is a partial order, and \\mu \\succeq \\lambda implies \\mu \\geq \\lambda. It follows from Theorem 5.15.1 and its proof that\n\n\\chi_\\lambda = \\sum_{\\mu \\succeq \\lambda} \\widetilde{K}_{\\mu\\lambda} \\chi_{U_\\mu},\n\nwhere (\\widetilde{K}_{\\lambda\\mu}) is the matrix inverse to the matrix of Kostka numbers (K_{\\lambda\\mu}). This implies that the Kostka numbers K_{\\mu\\lambda} vanish unless \\mu \\succeq \\lambda.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.15. The Frobenius character formula\u0009Partial order on partitions and vanishing of Kostka numbers","header":"Partial order on partitions and vanishing of Kostka numbers","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___15___-The-Frobenius-character-formula/Partial-order-on-partitions-and-vanishing-of-Kostka-numbers/#Chapter5___Remark5___15___5"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___23___-Algebraic-representations-of-___GL_LPAR_V_RPAR____/Algebraic-representations-and-highest-weight/#Chapter5___Discussion_after_Definition5___23___1___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.23. Algebraic representations of $GL(V)$\u0009Algebraic representations and highest weight\u0009Algebraic representations and highest weight","header":"Formalization","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___23___-Algebraic-representations-of-___GL_LPAR_V_RPAR____/Algebraic-representations-and-highest-weight/#Chapter5___Discussion_after_Definition5___23___1___formalization"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___8___-Induced-representations/Section-5___8___-Induced-representations-___-restriction-and-induction/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___8___-Induced-representations--Section-5___8___-Induced-representations-___-restriction-and-induction--Section-5___8___-Induced-representations-___-restriction-and-induction--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"The coinduced representation of a group on the corresponding equivariant function space.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Definition5.8.1; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Introduction_5.8; role=primary]\n\nThe representation of a group induced from a representation of a finite-index subgroup.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Definition5.8.1; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Introduction_5.8; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.8. Induced representations\u0009Section 5.8: Induced representations — restriction and induction\u0009Section 5.8: Induced representations — restriction and induction\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___8___-Induced-representations/Section-5___8___-Induced-representations-___-restriction-and-induction/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___8___-Induced-representations--Section-5___8___-Induced-representations-___-restriction-and-induction--Section-5___8___-Induced-representations-___-restriction-and-induction--Formalization--Primary-declarations"},"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___8___-Complexes-and-cohomology/Complex___-differentials___-cohomology___-exact-sequence/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--7___-Introduction-to-categories--7___8___-Complexes-and-cohomology--Complex___-differentials___-cohomology___-exact-sequence--Complex___-differentials___-cohomology___-exact-sequence--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"The type of integer-indexed cochain complexes in a category with zero morphisms.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter7/Definition7.8.1; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter7/Introduction_7.8; role=supporting]\n\nAn object assigned to an integer degree of a cochain complex in an abelian category.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter7/Definition7.8.1; role=supporting]* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter7/Introduction_7.8; role=supporting]\n\nA property of an integer-indexed cochain complex at a specified degree.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter7/Definition7.8.1; role=supporting]\n\nThe morphism from the component in one degree to the component in the succeeding degree.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter7/Definition7.8.1; role=supporting]\n\nA property of an integer-indexed cochain complex.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter7/Definition7.8.1; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00097. Introduction to categories\u00097.8. Complexes and cohomology\u0009Complex, differentials, cohomology, exact sequence\u0009Complex, differentials, cohomology, exact sequence\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/7___-Introduction-to-categories/7___8___-Complexes-and-cohomology/Complex___-differentials___-cohomology___-exact-sequence/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--7___-Introduction-to-categories--7___8___-Complexes-and-cohomology--Complex___-differentials___-cohomology___-exact-sequence--Complex___-differentials___-cohomology___-exact-sequence--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/8___-Homological-algebra/8___1___-Projective-and-injective-modules/Definition-8___1___8-___-Projective-and-injective-objects-in-abelian-categories/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--8___-Homological-algebra--8___1___-Projective-and-injective-modules--Definition-8___1___8-___-Projective-and-injective-objects-in-abelian-categories--Definition-8___1___8-___-Projective-and-injective-objects-in-abelian-categories--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"In an abelian category, the Coyoneda object property holds exactly when the associated\npreadditive Coyoneda functor preserves finite limits and finite colimits.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter8/Definition8.1.8; role=primary]\n\nIn an abelian category, the Yoneda object property holds exactly when the associated\npreadditive Yoneda functor preserves finite limits and finite colimits.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter8/Definition8.1.8; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00098. Homological algebra\u00098.1. Projective and injective modules\u0009Definition 8.1.8 — Projective and injective objects in abelian categories\u0009Definition 8.1.8 — Projective and injective objects in abelian categories\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/8___-Homological-algebra/8___1___-Projective-and-injective-modules/Definition-8___1___8-___-Projective-and-injective-objects-in-abelian-categories/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--8___-Homological-algebra--8___1___-Projective-and-injective-modules--Definition-8___1___8-___-Projective-and-injective-objects-in-abelian-categories--Definition-8___1___8-___-Projective-and-injective-objects-in-abelian-categories--Formalization--Primary-declarations"},"/8___-Homological-algebra/8___1___-Projective-and-injective-modules/Duality-between-projective-and-injective-modules/#Chapter8___Example8___1___7":{"contents":"Example 8.1.7. Let A be an algebra and P be a left A-module. Then P is projective if and only if P^* is an injective right A-module.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00098. Homological algebra\u00098.1. Projective and injective modules\u0009Duality between projective and injective modules","header":"Duality between projective and injective modules","id":"/8___-Homological-algebra/8___1___-Projective-and-injective-modules/Duality-between-projective-and-injective-modules/#Chapter8___Example8___1___7"},"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___2___-Projective-covers/Section-9___2___-Projective-covers/#Chapter9___Introduction_9___2___heading-1":{"contents":"Obviously, every finitely generated projective module over a finite dimensional algebra A is a direct sum of indecomposable projective modules, so to understand finitely generated projective modules over A, it suffices to classify indecomposable projective modules.\n\nLet A be a finite dimensional algebra with simple modules M_1, \\ldots, M_n.\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00099. Structure of finite dimensional algebras\u00099.2. Projective covers\u0009Section 9.2: Projective covers\u0009Section 9.2: Projective covers","header":"9.2. Projective covers","id":"/9___-Structure-of-finite-dimensional-algebras/9___2___-Projective-covers/Section-9___2___-Projective-covers/#Chapter9___Introduction_9___2___heading-1"}});